# Line endings, for the two cases where getting them wrong is silent rather than loud.
#
# A shell script checked out with CRLF dies as "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M", which at
# least says so. A list of paths read by a shell loop is the quiet one: `read` leaves the
# carriage return on every entry, each `rm` then matches nothing, and the script reports success
# having removed none of them. The release tooling strips `\r` defensively as well; this is what
# makes it impossible rather than handled.
*.sh text eol=lf
tools/release/export-exclude.txt text eol=lf
